The root cause of the failure was a logical error in `index.js` where the `Number.isFinite` check for strings was negated (`!Number.isFinite(+num)`), causing it to return `false` for valid numeric strings and `true` for non-numeric strings.

I have corrected this by removing the negation operator.

```javascript
// Before
return Number.isFinite ? !Number.isFinite(+num) : isFinite(+num);

// After

The issue was in the `run` function within `index.js`. The code was explicitly catching errors and resolving the caller's promise with `undefined` instead of propagating the original promise:

```javascript
// Before
resolve(result.catch(() => undefined));
```

This caused the caller to receive `undefined` even when the limited function rejected, which broke the "does not ignore errors" test case.

The issue was caused by a broken guard in `index.js` where the check for whether a preserved character equals the separator was explicitly disabled with `false &&`. I have removed the `false &&` to restore the error throwing behavior.

```javascript
// index.js
- if (false && character === options.separator) {
+ if (character === options.separator) {
```

All tests now pass.
